{"objects":[{"id":"flow","instance":"instance--b96dad5f-c519-5a1f-a718-912ca4a86c02","objects":["attack-action--1","attack-action--2","attack-action--3","attack-action--4","attack-action--5","attack-action--6","attack-action--7","attack-action--8","attack-condition--1","attack-condition--2","attack-condition--3","software--1","file--1","instance--35651ad7-74e3-5ba2-bff9-d25f075a3e4b","process--1","instance--c2fc13aa-739c-543f-9d9f-73c1b618cc81","process--2","file--2","instance--ad1f125b-7701-5e9d-b869-79f40471648d","instance--384798bd-a84b-5feb-8d38-7f9d0a087f11","instance--ed6039fe-678b-5ae9-9576-73e6ea35b0c8","instance--a6618e5f-2d2d-55c4-9d60-5edfe63f3ef7","instance--b6d82c28-0a68-5c6f-b058-c1e072f51852","instance--2ed631bc-5855-5c59-80fa-4925353c85a4","instance--56b40d44-cc57-5e8b-b320-779a685089fd","instance--56da8838-1109-5fce-9d89-7b3ec32d7010","instance--7cc46cbd-beb8-54eb-aef6-32656d039f20","instance--3ace54e2-1a43-5724-944b-c4b01d63d9e6","instance--bb170793-a0b8-5a22-b9f9-5dff26468abb","instance--e7ff83c4-c2b5-5db0-b953-761ea46af0b4","instance--77e5b927-a6a8-51b7-afbf-c8e2095e9bc8","instance--d65a4028-8d7e-50b3-af5f-7e71c76bb85c","instance--e2bf2e72-cbdb-51c1-9935-1ec0a591e6ba","instance--c8bf660c-d32e-52ca-a5c1-54ac879c4a87","instance--127334cb-1b57-5763-a2bb-d549a3e939f1"],"properties":[["name","H1N1: Technical analysis reveals new capabilities"],["description","This blog is the first in a that will provide an in-depth technical analysis on the H1N1 malware. I\u2019ll be looking at how H1N1 has evolved, its\u00a0obfuscation, analyzing its execution including new information stealing and user account control bypass capabilities, and finally exploring how we are both using and influencing security tools with this research."],["scope","malware"],["author",[["name","Software Engineer"]]],["external_references",[["item-1",[["source_name","blogs.cisco.com"],["url","http://blogs.cisco.com/security/h1n1-technical-analysis-reveals-new-capabilities"]]]]]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--ad592d05-6969-5b51-98a8-1ad9b6aab896","120":"instance--5850a8f4-8241-5e73-9bf0-dcd376cd5025","150":"instance--0da1498f-3beb-5916-a249-e0410a4a81e4","180":"instance--d3632784-7391-5a29-83e2-edab51db56c6","210":"instance--8bc47ca5-ca73-5a04-a9ea-918608f10871","240":"instance--6546eef6-c5ad-516e-a417-89916e651648","270":"instance--244e5a69-f465-564e-9d2c-93d4da44fc57","30":"instance--6a1fca3c-fdb6-536a-bbea-61ac2349a700","300":"instance--043a5b44-2864-5565-abce-1909204e5485","330":"instance--e10eb918-2b0b-56f4-9c82-f345ce21276c","60":"instance--33a99d1b-dad8-51f6-be74-e1614db9151b","90":"instance--380c79e9-b1eb-547c-bf01-a011db1201c1"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--1","properties":[["name","Spearphishing delivers malicious document"],["description","Unsurprisingly, these documents are delivered through spear-phishing e-mail campaigns."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0001","technique":"T1566.001"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--4f4d03a8-4502-5010-997b-9943e254395b","120":"instance--cefe62ac-5b6e-5ead-84fc-b4c632913a73","150":"instance--4d07a740-2467-5b95-ac8d-4a2ee8edd11e","180":"instance--1205f03c-a457-5a0b-87ef-3bbb7aac110f","210":"instance--96e7dc6a-9f86-5f69-8d45-a566303cd4c0","240":"instance--66237af6-bb19-5a51-9fa2-cbc4a96186f5","270":"instance--836dfd42-b475-50ca-9acf-4f11bd32b89f","30":"instance--efe92997-cc5c-538a-90bc-c09ffb1379f4","300":"instance--9c4a24ae-1426-5cb5-9d7c-792e6dd325b7","330":"instance--06c6a74a-ca7d-5703-88ea-a06a0933d21a","60":"instance--5e4f84e5-56b7-5b8f-9bc3-b4d59d371123","90":"instance--019deb6a-bab3-57db-9a53-11e3564bf7a4"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--2","properties":[["name","Social engineer macro execution"],["description","Upon opening the document, the attacker attempts to social engineer the user into executing the malicious macro content by stating it will adjust to their version of Microsoft Word:"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1204.002"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0ce35aea-35a2-52ae-a67b-985306bdefe7","120":"instance--312639d5-e6d9-57b1-850d-28f0a1eac2f6","150":"instance--9551a25e-2558-512e-9894-dd64a8d662bf","180":"instance--403becce-e0ad-5dcd-bc14-7c00453907da","210":"instance--2305f501-2712-5ec6-9d4e-1371b011e937","240":"instance--cf1b8cf6-1cbd-5afb-b653-aa693889dab5","270":"instance--1d3dc59a-40a0-5670-aa3e-1304ff762003","30":"instance--77063d4f-503a-5e9b-b622-15aa2cc6a8e7","300":"instance--beaab67f-8a54-5982-8c5d-9d2ed2d42c9f","330":"instance--4d3274ff-ef14-5a53-99be-01575194a98e","60":"instance--4154a6bf-290b-5fce-a557-5dc0f3e700fb","90":"instance--2992bc0b-df57-54e5-a17b-03a964338c09"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--3","properties":[["name","Extract and decode embedded executable"],["description","As mentioned, the binary to be executed is extracted from a VBA form text box:\n\nThe text box content is set into a variable, which is then passed off to a de-obfuscation function. The core de-obfuscation functionality is a two steps process. The first is an XOR loop with a fixed byte key of 0xE, which produces a base64 encoded portable executable (PE):\n\nThe second is a VBA implementation of base64 that decodes it to produce a final Portable Executable (PE):"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1027"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--0f6e4da7-a5f4-54d7-866f-3bc6e8bcb019","120":"instance--015254b5-0906-577e-b3e3-3899f837d118","150":"instance--f7af5b8c-330a-58d5-b0da-a3d5f0bc6ad0","180":"instance--9f27ab21-2382-567d-b022-74ad5a29bd08","210":"instance--d799eb0a-7600-5b1a-a298-c47a78ceae4b","240":"instance--911c2c52-5974-5e08-b0b6-a9084d57c6ee","270":"instance--286444b4-97a1-58cd-9ca3-cffa04a15351","30":"instance--92192c38-5143-50e0-8c39-a96ea86e32e2","300":"instance--82bb3de0-bea5-5651-a1de-583283258c6d","330":"instance--4f0945c8-f644-50ce-8796-fea52a29dda1","60":"instance--16c43a2d-7356-521d-a507-5922e2c3337b","90":"instance--abce5c62-339a-5ed5-a0b8-dd66136cfe2f"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--4","properties":[["name","Write executable to temp and execute"],["description","The de-obfuscated executable is then written to %temp% and executed. We can follow the execution flow through the use of process visualization in AMP Threat Grid. What this provides is graphed process interactions (child-parent relationships) for the entirety of the run. In the case of the H1N1 malicious document, it is very apparent that WINWORD.EXE is executing a separate binary:"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0002","technique":"T1059.005"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--a469636c-0cb2-50b9-92f9-644c8aa98197","120":"instance--d57b3a21-e910-5879-8148-40165cea451a","150":"instance--58250ef4-5bb4-5bf3-b33e-89493bbb395d","180":"instance--3e769cf2-c1e5-5d2e-81d4-892f48ce4d86","210":"instance--f6499e41-749c-5d7f-9346-96c08d0d5dfd","240":"instance--d9dcdfc8-5f42-546a-803a-0efcd5d4cf91","270":"instance--c9afd0f6-97ce-57b2-9d60-3557b6214809","30":"instance--75f82155-f5ff-5619-8bf8-aae22937cab0","300":"instance--25e2d26e-1b35-56bd-b9aa-096c2d3b0e3c","330":"instance--e0483688-da10-5376-8cf2-050bcc3b70f0","60":"instance--1293d9cc-16b2-5c67-a453-bd144aab133a","90":"instance--2b43cd52-10db-5b15-8ad5-c4b59496528b"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--5","properties":[["name","Inject unpacked code into suspended temp process"],["description","The binary has a total of three routines responsible for unpacking and injection. The first routine injects via the following steps:\n\nUnpacking algorithm unpacks code to be written\n\nCreates a suspended process of the executable written to %temp% from the document with CreateProcessA\n\nWrites to that image with WriteProcessMemory\n\nUses GetThreatContext, SetThreadContext and ResumeThread to execute at the EP of the unpacked executable. On the call to WriteProcessMemory we see the lpBuffer address points to a complete PE, as is indicated by the MZ header:"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055.012"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--54fc1f38-f4e3-523e-b07c-73889b0d8df7","120":"instance--9cf45948-7040-5d7a-a1d5-3cb4db0c1af6","150":"instance--13bb13cc-e02b-51d2-8d7d-40e5613b6e5a","180":"instance--e33bc6ee-771e-54fe-a772-74dfdaad420d","210":"instance--286f51a3-8fc6-55ec-978e-8b05ebda3655","240":"instance--14659696-be77-5460-beb1-4b5eac126e43","270":"instance--f48ed1a6-2895-5cd2-9b1c-e30ac2bc8921","30":"instance--7b803e61-16ac-5720-92cd-8f2a456a52b7","300":"instance--76c24361-5143-5c43-8e4d-707c619e6038","330":"instance--f395cfc7-395a-5001-9552-a4fec6dbc53a","60":"instance--760885bb-9294-5a5b-93a2-a410e1ea4112","90":"instance--8e0d1a12-8611-598d-9a7d-40f2525e4781"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--6","properties":[["name","Hollow explorer process with unpacked image"],["description","The next routine makes use of the injection method used by Duqu to write its unpacked image :\n\nCreateProcessW is called to create a suspended \u2018Explorer.exe\u2019 process\n\nUse the handle from PROCESS_INFORMATION produced by CreateProcessW with ZwQueryInformationProcess to get Explorer.exe PEB and ImageBaseAddress\n\nAllocate and write up to 500 bytes of of the Explorer.exe process using ReadProcessMemory\n\nGet actual image size from PE header, allocated this size, and write entire Explorer.exe image into memory\n\nUse UnMapViewOfSection with ImageBaseAddress and process handle of Explorer.exe from step 2 to un-map the current section in order to avoid STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES upon mapping of the new section\n\nOverwrite image sections of Explorer.exe with unpacked (of the current step) executable code\n\nUse MapViewOfSection to map the manipulated Explorer.exe using the process handle from step 2\n\nCall ResumeThread to start execution of unpacked code (of the current step)"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055.012"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--7cccbcd0-a724-54fb-be4f-ac523d26f7d0","120":"instance--738e44b2-1357-5eb3-82bd-8a0ce8942b72","150":"instance--d31f4735-6339-5929-8d7c-d30863cf166f","180":"instance--2252393d-d801-5931-ad38-4b53374a9880","210":"instance--065b5ad5-023a-5336-b458-e9c9dcda2976","240":"instance--5ea0dd2b-5eac-51f8-855a-4cbcb801bc2d","270":"instance--bba66874-a069-56c6-bb95-967cb3cefc9e","30":"instance--6377c09d-ecb4-5567-a2f4-4dde56ff4f98","300":"instance--ede3430f-b16b-58ae-be71-1d1f1a194336","330":"instance--7772f482-8ee2-569a-b600-7528c065d4e0","60":"instance--98139a98-16b0-54fe-8c77-3473c1b520cd","90":"instance--5cb9cc95-e190-5a68-985d-0a94d39299e6"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--7","properties":[["name","Write DLL into allocated memory"],["description","Breaking on the first VirtualAlloc performed by the injected process enabled us to see a large allocation occur, and setting a breakpoint on writing to this memory location makes it apparent that an entire DLL is written to this memory location by the (current) unpacking code:"],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1055"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--5150f7cc-6f25-5eb3-a6fc-5099ced4caf4","120":"instance--64a408ce-c543-55d6-a9dd-206ce7cadbb2","150":"instance--1b71e413-cfd2-528c-8792-dc7dd1bb9938","180":"instance--aa12ce11-0165-52b6-b72d-e4d9070e9fbb","210":"instance--5d8b0f08-14bc-58f4-9316-b25a92dd09a6","240":"instance--34f96b84-9453-53f2-ae07-d28c04665e6c","270":"instance--415f1d54-1cd4-5ba7-8071-4aa99bc946ac","30":"instance--18834cac-b4c4-57b0-88ea-5a62fdb27cf9","300":"instance--de4f9957-1ac6-5782-86ea-f1a3b6a9ad13","330":"instance--8eff940b-86bb-556f-8c85-c7c47577b3ed","60":"instance--14a123fd-5626-5e69-8257-6dcd64c28ec0","90":"instance--02294ff1-7261-5db8-8e0d-b9e818922053"},"id":"action","instance":"attack-action--8","properties":[["name","Resolve imports dynamically by hashing"],["description","Upon opening the binary in a disassembler (in this case IDA Pro) we see that imports are resolved dynamically using hashing of DLLs and exports, and a string obfuscation technique used throughout the binary."],["confidence",0.0],["ttp",{"tactic":"TA0005","technique":"T1027.007"}]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--35652c79-2c41-5d60-81ba-b5e47aa554bf","120":"instance--53906203-fffb-5af3-b112-44a9b4b49b8f","150":"instance--465fbc8a-7b4c-569f-be00-c0fbc137fcfc","180":"instance--a653c96b-3673-5dfa-968e-188fffe4cc04","210":"instance--6a344cf5-bd5c-52db-84f5-23c7701c7a5e","30":"instance--913561d1-b5f3-54d2-8272-159233dfde54","330":"instance--d59fe52d-c897-5f3a-919a-c2273cb8a8b3","60":"instance--f1fd6a3d-bb83-543d-a379-39d4632978f7","90":"instance--4cd6fce2-0d24-57f0-b161-12a867cd1100","branch:False":"instance--f7e897b3-3607-5ea5-8d15-5f83ba8a60da","branch:True":"instance--39b5ac19-b0dd-5674-a7d9-d75e659fe9ea"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--1","properties":[["description","Upon opening the document, the attacker attempts to social engineer the user into executing the malicious macro content by stating it will adjust to their version of Microsoft Word:"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--2ff8b105-17d8-5bc8-9736-a71ca9281068","120":"instance--fe9b11f7-9a07-51c8-93b2-02f34148833b","150":"instance--15f0676c-2f2f-58cf-99a3-b5eade2d286e","180":"instance--cdc5e3d9-a0b4-5995-98a3-d1a29532ffb1","210":"instance--75ed5df8-ff2f-5667-9748-b8587a1ee318","30":"instance--46009917-4258-54e5-9e91-c46c4a45d34d","330":"instance--54845373-a5f0-5af0-b348-5b0a3b4fbe67","60":"instance--f1f8d9a5-1f2a-55c4-af9a-f2629bc0827a","90":"instance--ddfb6e54-4af9-5771-910f-b4c7ba6e9860","branch:False":"instance--64607b7d-a4bd-56e7-9116-7bc21201c8bf","branch:True":"instance--f635d174-aa29-531f-b9c8-0cc481c6c1dd"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--2","properties":[["description","In the case of the H1N1 malicious document, it is very apparent that WINWORD.EXE is executing a separate binary:"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--64fa8274-9976-53b9-811c-a0e7e805b799","120":"instance--ce384754-4013-5515-ac17-4456e1d80bbb","150":"instance--99275651-db0d-57ec-a13c-6e557a4b9c7c","180":"instance--cbaa25ac-4f14-51f0-9962-f76fe36e7cd6","210":"instance--1c4fda5b-4605-5040-a88a-48f1df4144aa","30":"instance--39743932-1858-5655-a859-b27c0902b87a","330":"instance--c116df79-3c10-5931-96ec-25b4174ae13a","60":"instance--75b926aa-d189-5cd6-bc7b-5a947c88de41","90":"instance--4931e63d-9e52-5183-bbd8-7db1814b0f21","branch:False":"instance--fc00a7ae-0a9a-58b7-b24a-5def4ea9feab","branch:True":"instance--e3424864-637f-5b96-b87c-a8dcc22d8b9e"},"id":"condition","instance":"attack-condition--3","properties":[["description","Breaking on the first VirtualAlloc performed by the injected process enabled us to see a large allocation occur, and setting a breakpoint on writing to this memory location makes it apparent that an entire DLL is written to this memory location by the (current) unpacking code:"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--905f37ff-524a-522a-8685-08b6df9b34bd","120":"instance--a0b8bf42-8310-53d9-87f0-390378638ae9","150":"instance--ffafa2fa-6430-5714-9c5e-bbf977c9819b","180":"instance--10bf0e4d-ac4d-5742-b82b-398ef2467e2a","210":"instance--f2066b53-6fa4-5efa-8f61-dc81d3df5b0e","240":"instance--fbb6b151-c9f4-5cb6-b467-c563cc0c60d3","270":"instance--451ff5e8-5cc8-5d6c-b701-da17c04936f7","30":"instance--059bc992-da53-5d4b-ac82-60cfacc08e81","300":"instance--1d241382-a8ee-554a-aa3a-04366535b315","330":"instance--8a7078e9-787d-56b2-a734-f93cd76b3dc8","60":"instance--8aab1831-f6e3-582d-8f9b-3ad920a40e35","90":"instance--192458e5-76c8-5d8c-ab54-9ed8b36ef2bd"},"id":"software","instance":"software--1","properties":[["evidence",[["item-1",{"excerpt":"Upon opening the document, the attacker attempts to social engineer the user into executing the malicious macro content by stating it will adjust to their version of Microsoft Word:","source":"http://blogs.cisco.com/security/h1n1-technical-analysis-reveals-new-capabilities"}]]],["display_name","Microsoft Word"],["name","Microsoft Word"]]},{"anchors":{"0":"instance--8cf549a5-7e7d-51cf-9368-9b5e0744592a","120":"instance--06d4fda